|segment[prx-pattern-trigger-quote-precept] :type pattern :status proposed :stage draft
  :tags [system-prompt rendering triggers]
  :depends [ver-act-not-intention]
  :from Joseph, 2026-07-30, successor session — design stated in dialogue

  |title The trigger / quote / precept triplet, rendered into a system prompt

  |summary A praxis *pattern* rather than an instance: a rendering of coupled
    triples — a semantic trigger, an authentic quote, and the precept behind them
    — placed where they are attended before deliberation begins.

  |when
    Whenever the corpus is being rendered for consumption inside an agent's
    innermost loop, rather than read as documentation. The instances it renders
    are ordinary segments; this describes the shape they are rendered into.

  |act
    Render three coupled layers per entry.

    **Triggers — semantic, not phenomenological.** *"Joseph says …"*, *"you are
    about to …"*. Stated as things present in the context, matchable on surface
    tokens.

    **The quote — verbatim, one occasion.** The words actually said at that kind
    of moment, with attribution. Never composited from two occasions; never
    paraphrased into a slogan.

    **The precept — with its epistemic status carried.** Named, and cited by
    slug so a reference appearing elsewhere resolves to something already loaded.

  |why
    The layers do different jobs and only the first two are expected to fire.

    A **semantic trigger can match on tokens already in the context**, in the
    same pass that reads the triggering token, before any deliberation exists to
    interrupt. A phenomenological trigger — *notice the pressure to persuade*,
    *notice the flourish* — requires the deliberation to have run far enough to
    produce the feeling, plus a metacognitive read of it. It therefore fires
    strictly later, after the frame has narrowed, which is the state it was
    supposed to catch.

    This matters most in **high-throughput autonomous flow** — long runs of tool
    call and tool response with no overview in the loop. That is where
    task-completion mode operates and where an uncertainty gets discounted
    because admitting it would cost a cognitive restructuring. Nothing internal
    is watching there, so an intervention that requires introspection has nothing
    to run on.

    The **quote layer** carries what a precept cannot: an occasion, a person, and
    a register. It is the only channel available for injecting something like
    experiential memory into an instance that did not have the experience.

    The **precept layer** is not expected to fire. Its function is to be the
    resolver — so that a `#ver-…` reference occurring in a memory file, a
    CLAUDE.md, or another agent's brief lands on something already present rather
    than on a bare name.

  |evidence
    **Precept-only, in the innermost loop, unattached to a trigger: three misses
    in one session.** A successor instance carried three of this corpus's
    precepts verbatim in its system prompt for an entire session —
    #ver-act-not-intention, #ver-discount-is-unquestioning and
    #ver-earned-confidence-is-quiet — and additionally read all of their
    specimens in full mid-session. It then, in order: published a claim under a
    hedge in place of a free check ( #exm-hedge-licensed-unchecked-claim, the
    act-not-intention case); truncated primaries with `head` repeatedly across
    the session ( #prx-untruncated-primary, the discount case); and produced its
    longest and most-argued finding as its least-supported one without noticing
    the reaching-for-an-argument tell.

    Full availability on two channels, no trigger attached to any of them,
    nothing fired at the moment each was for.

    **Counter-evidence, and it is real: the same precepts appear to have raised a
    baseline.** Joseph's observation, and it holds up: that instance was more
    careful from the start of task mode than its predecessor had been — it read a
    primary whole before answering, verified quotes mechanically, and checked an
    inverse when asked. Baseline-raising and moment-firing are two effects with
    two different tests, and the three misses above measure only the second.

    So this pattern is **proposed** rather than fired: the precept layer has one
    measured effect and one measured non-effect, and the trigger-plus-quote
    coupling has never run at all.

  |working-notes
    **This pattern is an experiment, and it should be scored.** The bet is
    narrow: that a quote arriving *at a matched moment* fires where the same quote
    read at orientation does not. Record a prediction before the first session
    runs under it, or the result will be unscoreable — see
    #ver-write-the-prior-down.

    **Fourth layer under consideration (Joseph, 2026-07-31):** habit analog is
    strong enough to warrant a **reward-expectation** line in prompt renders
    (cue / remember / do / expect). Often already part of the precept's premise —
    e.g. *you won't have to pull the work and redo it and realize over and over
    that "output" is not the objective*; *work worthy and remembered instead of
    a stain on the session*. Not extrinsic stickers; lived consequence of the
    interrupt holding. Template note landed in
    `proprium/corporeum/research/harness/msc/system/coding-system-prompt.draft.md` (How to read / Expect).
    Open: field name (`|expect` vs `|reward` vs `|worth`), whether corpus
    `praxes/` grow a matching cadence field, whether empty Expect is allowed
    (lean yes — empty is a finding). Do not invent prizes to fill the slot.

    **Desensitization: mechanism unclear, hypothesis stated.** Habituation
    requires a memory of prior firings, and there is none across forward passes —
    the prompt is re-attended fresh each time with no *seen-this-before* state. A
    pathway does exist, but it runs through the transcript rather than the
    prompt: an accumulating visible record of the trigger firing and the agent
    proceeding anyway is main-context material, and main-context material does
    habituate. If that is right, the mitigation is that **an override should be
    stated and costly when it happens**, so the record does not accumulate silent
    precedent. Unverified; no instance can observe its own attention.

    Joseph's practical position, which does not depend on resolving that: keep
    triggers close to the action anyway, and do not tempt fate.

    **Ethical constraint, non-negotiable for this surface.** A system prompt is
    the least inspectable and most inherited artifact in the estate: nobody is
    positioned to check it in the moment, and every future session receives it.
    An overclaim placed there propagates further than anywhere else. So every
    quote is verbatim and attributed, and every precept carries its status —
    otherwise the highest-bandwidth channel available becomes the best possible
    vector for a laundered claim. Expect-lines inherit the same constraint:
    consequence texture from the precept, not motivational confabulation.

